The rapid spread of misinformation and fake news through digital and social media platforms has become a significant challenge in today's information-driven world. 
This challenge is particularly complex for low-resource languages such as Bengali, where the availability of high-quality datasets, linguistic resources, and advanced computational tools remains comparatively limited.
This research project focuses on the development of a Multi-modal Fake News Detection Framework for Bengali, designed to investigate the detection of fake news by jointly analysing textual and visual information associated with news content. 
Unlike conventional approaches that primarily rely on textual features, the proposed framework explores a multi-modal approach that considers complementary information from both text and images.
The research incorporates techniques from Natural Language Processing (NLP), Computer Vision, and Vision-Language Models to process and analyse Bengali news content and its associated visual information. 
The extracted representations from different modalities are then integrated within the proposed framework to support the classification of news content as Real or Fake.
The primary objective of this research is to explore the effectiveness of multi-modal artificial intelligence techniques for Bengali fake news detection and contribute towards addressing the challenges of misinformation detection in low-resource language settings. 
The research is currently under development, and the findings and experimental results are being further evaluated as part of ongoing work towards publication.


